(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["ArrayObject", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"c8d9c57a-9a78-544b-933e-57153bed408f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"ArrayObject", "[", "data", "]"}], "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"00047d5c-d988-6a47-b4c5-ddba47a6bb08"],
 " ",
 "represents the array with entries data."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"54d4b60e-5cb8-b54f-b772-5e0c1b8cd81f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"ArrayObject", "[", 
    RowBox[{"data", ",", " ", "sym"}], "]"}], "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"7830c60a-439a-3c4f-8c2d-a1097e391f42"],
 " ",
 "additionally declares the index symmetry sym."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"7095c1c5-49aa-2c43-9a91-4379d58ae1c6"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"ab99d4cc-f9ed-6448-9d06-44f1a2cb21d6"],

Cell["\<\
The entries data must form a rectangular array. A ragged list is not one.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"69934ac8-07ce-2c4b-8c5c-555e4462b83d"],

Cell["\<\
The entries themselves may be anything at all: numbers, elements of a finite \
field, symbolic expressions, labels from a dictionary. Nothing about the \
structure of an array depends on what sits in it, so there is one array \
object rather than one per kind of entry.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"46633738-b27f-3744-8fdf-bded166ded5f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "What the array is made of is read off the entries by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["ArrayDomain", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ba9fbda0-02e5-4b48-a89f-cc7bd7bb3095"],
 " rather than declared alongside them, and an array whose entries answer to \
no recognized domain is an array of expressions."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"d79ce630-6bc7-8743-bb11-d20525f31584"],

Cell["\<\
A scalar is accepted as the array of order zero. It has no indices and a \
single entry, and it is what a full contraction leaves behind and what \
multiplies an array entry by entry, so scalar multiplication is nothing but \
ArrayTimes with a 0-array.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"60a596d8-a5aa-224a-80d2-cba5bd757745"],

Cell["\<\
The index symmetry sym is one of \"Rigid\" (the default), \"Cyclic\" or \
\"Symmetric\". The spelling is case-insensitive on input, but the stored form \
is always one of the three.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"e57e2851-ddb9-0647-93fe-95138c0bfe36"],

Cell["\<\
A declared symmetry is checked against the entries, and rejected with \
ArrayObject::nosym if they are not invariant under the corresponding index \
permutations. Only a regular array can carry a symmetry other than \"Rigid\". \
The symmetry is the one thing an array is told rather than asked, because it \
is a claim about the entries that they may or may not bear out.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"aeab4d24-686d-2045-8082-dc51b552f8bd"],

Cell["\<\
An array displays as a summary box. Normal gives its entries, and \
ArrayGraphics draws them.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"97e1d461-f066-474a-a7e5-49a930d2e1af"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"0811435b-c6d5-ac40-872d-ca934768ff10"],

Cell["A vector, a matrix and a 3-array:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"49fb3971-3cf6-644b-8ad2-ab3de8e93a5c"],

Cell["\<\
{ArrayObject[{1, 2, 3}], ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}],
 ArrayObject[Array[#1 + #2 + #3 &, {2, 2, 2}]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"d7fbc2c9-3f31-684b-adef-17efa7d00838"],

